WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.467 --> 00:00:07.060
PRADEEP SAWLANI:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.550
My name is Pradeep Sawlani.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.260
I'm an Engineering
Manager focused

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.030
on simplifying authentication
for Anthos customers.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:15.750
RICHARD LIU: My
name is Richard Liu,

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.460
and I'm a Product
Manager focused

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.490
on streamlining the user
experience for authentication

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.710
for Anthos customers.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.570
Authentication is complex.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:28.360
Passwords have progressed
to keys, certificates,

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.100
and multi-factor auth.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.710
Basic authentication
has shifted to

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:36.910
advanced, more-secure protocols,
such as Kerberos, LDAP, SAML,

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:38.290
and OAuth.

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:40.450
Single-tenant
monolithic applications

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:43.990
have evolved to multi-tenant
containerized applications,

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:47.530
distributed across hybrid
and multi-cloud environments.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.180
It's clear that
authentication has

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:52.510
become increasingly complex,
yet, more than ever critical

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.120
to get right.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.370
Ensuring a secure and integrated
authentication experience

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:00.140
requires both time
and expertise.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.660
This leads to
on-boarding friction,

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:05.230
as enterprises shift workloads
to containers in the cloud,

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.870
causing delays in
modernization plans.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:10.390
On the other hand,
security risks

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.580
have far greater implications.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.380
Multiple authentication
solutions,

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.600
for example,
maintaining certificates

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.240
alongside protocols
such as OIDC,

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.760
may increase the chance
of security breaches.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.030
And improper access control
can lead to misconfigurations

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:26.740
and vulnerabilities.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.700
In fact, in a recent
survey, 78% of organizations

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.490
cite security, not
cost, as top priority

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:36.010
when deploying
identity solutions.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:37.300
What's the end result?

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.000
Administrators
spend excessive time

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.740
on deploying
authentication solutions

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:46.190
and preventing misconfigurations
in their environment.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:49.220
The complexity is taken further
with hybrid and multi-cloud

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:50.240
environments.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.460
As enterprises modernize
their applications

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:55.100
and adopt solutions
such Anthos, they'll

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.590
be faced with a fragmented
identity challenge

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.780
across multiple environments.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:02.750
Each environment is built on
a different identity store.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.930
For example, Active
Directory for on-prem,

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:11.210
Cloud Identity for Google Cloud,
and IAM for AWS and Azure.

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:14.360
Many of these environments may
use the same identity provider,

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.480
or maintain a bespoke
identity solution.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.330
For example, we have some
groups in other clouds using

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:24.410
IAM accounts, users in on-prem
using both Okta and Active

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.230
Directory, and users in
other clouds connecting

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:28.940
to Active Directory.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:32.120
Mergers and acquisitions
add to the complexity.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.010
In addition to
employee identity,

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.970
customers also need to manage
partner, end user, and workload

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:39.170
identity.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:42.620
Essentially, the identity
solution is a mess.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.260
So how do we enable customers
to manage their clusters

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.440
across multiple environments,
each with a different identity

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.900
provider and protocol?

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:54.440
During this session, we'll walk
through two separate workflows.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.230
Users authenticating to
the Anthos Control Plane,

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:01.070
and workloads running on Anthos
clusters, accessing, GCP APIs

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:02.880
and services.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:06.570
Let's start with
user authentication.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:09.450
Meet Charlie, who's a
Platform Administrator.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.760
Charlie manages a
cross-functional team

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.490
of developers focused on
building and maintaining

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:17.610
IT platforms within
their organization.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.590
Charlie's team maintains
two separate environments,

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:24.150
GKE on-prem, and GKE on AWS.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.240
He needs to ensure his team
has proper and secure access

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.360
to the Kubernetes API
Server to perform management

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.330
and troubleshooting tasks.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:36.090
Charlie is faced with
highly-complex identity

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.350
requirements.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.440
His organization has already
started modernization efforts

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.740
by shifting on-prem workloads
to containers, which

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.600
operate around-the-clock
to support

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.380
both internal and
customer-facing applications.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:51.660
Within his team,
Alex is a developer

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.330
working on their
internal HR application,

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:58.230
and is provisioned within
Active Directory on-premises.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.830
Alex needs Administrative
access to GKE on-prem clusters

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.200
to perform management tasks.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.420
With the recent
acquisition of a startup,

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.320
Charlie's company
brought in developers,

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.000
such as Blake, that are
maintaining projects on AWS,

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.770
with identities stored in Okta.

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:17.550
Blake needs access to
a customer-facing SaaS

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.829
application to perform
troubleshooting

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:23.070
on GKE on AWS clusters.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.930
And there's other
employees maintained

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.970
in different identity
solutions that

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:29.730
further add to the complexity.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.180
So how do Administrators
such as Charlie

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:35.710
manage such complex
identity environments?

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:39.390
Let's take a look
at some options.

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:41.670
What are some
options for identity?

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.220
Charlie can use Kubernetes
Identity, starting

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.320
with client certificates.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.570
However, this is
insecure, due to the need

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.730
to maintain private
keys that are typically

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.080
handled on insecure media.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.350
Anyone with access
to these repositories

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:59.460
may gain unwarranted
access to the cluster.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.950
Charlie may also opt
for a basic auth,

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:04.770
but that's mostly limited to
test and dev environments,

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:08.040
since credentials never expire,
and passwords cannot change

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.030
without restarting
the API Server.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:12.980
There are also service
account tokens,

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:15.140
which present risks
as well, with users

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:18.050
that access secrets and can
authenticate as a service

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:19.740
account.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.500
We recommend Open
ID Connect, or OIDC

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:25.410
for short, given the
long-lived-- short-lived and

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:27.080
refreshed tokens.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.700
But it's not available
for every environment.

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:34.230
Where is it currently
available for Anthos?

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.110
Let's start with on-prem.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:39.080
Customers can already leverage
external OIDC Identity

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:41.930
providers, such as
ADFS, to authenticate

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:44.570
to GKE on-prem clusters.

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:47.240
We currently have
GA support for OIDC,

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:51.110
and Alpha support for
LDAP for GKE on-prem.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:54.260
We've also recently launched
a beta for OIDC access

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:58.940
to GKE on AWS clusters, and are
looking to add support for GKE

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:01.660
on GCP in the future.

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:03.620
Traditionally, Google
Cloud has offered

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:06.200
a synchronization-based
identity model,

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:09.950
requiring you to synchronize
identities with Google Cloud.

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:12.350
While this may work
for certain scenarios,

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:15.230
there may be concerns around
consistency and latency

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:18.170
for a hybrid or
multiple-cloud deployment.

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:20.480
With Anthos, we want to
meet you where you are,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.300
and enable you to use
third-party identity providers

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:25.580
and authentication protocols.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:27.650
You no longer need to
synchronize identities

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:30.110
with Google Cloud, or
worry about consistency

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:32.100
across environments.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.160
We want to save you
time and reduce friction

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:36.423
during the on-boarding process.

00:06:36.423 --> 00:06:38.090
Let's take a look at
how this will work,

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:42.020
with a demo starting
with GKE on-prem.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:44.340
This demo will walk through
three user journeys.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.310
Charlie will configure an
OIDC identity provider Active

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:49.290
Directory Federation
Services, also

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:51.840
known as ADFS, for his
team to authenticate

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:53.910
to a GKE on-prem cluster.

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:55.680
Alex will authenticate
using the G Cloud

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.620
CLI without requiring a
Cloud Identity account,

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:01.350
with the G Cloud Anthos
Auth Login command.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:04.050
Alex will also authenticate
from the Cloud Console.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.720
While this demo focuses
only on GKE on-prem,

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.720
the user experience will be the
same for any Anthos cluster,

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:10.800
including Anthos on AWS.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.560
Before getting
started, Charlie needs

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:17.030
to perform a few steps to
enable OIDC authentication.

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:19.680
He'll need to reach out to his
Organization Administrator, who

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.770
manages Active Directory
Domain Services, and provision

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:27.330
an additional server for ADFS,
which enables OIDC support.

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:29.430
As you can see, Alex and
Blake are provisioned

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:32.850
as users in the ADFS instance
in members of the same group,

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:34.390
Platform Developers.

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:37.270
The Administrator
registers an OIDC client

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:39.700
in ADFS with two redirect URLs.

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.860
One for Cloud Console
and one for G Cloud CLI.

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:44.760
The Administrator
may customize claims

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.760
to include group membership
information in tokens for use

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:49.930
with Kubernetes' RBAC policies.

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:52.410
The Administrator provides
Charlie with OIDC client

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.710
details, and Charlie proceeds
to create an Anthos cluster

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:59.130
and configure OIDC in the User
Cluster Configuration file

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:00.870
on an Admin Workstation.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:02.880
Let's take a look at this file.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.520
There are few fields that
are important for OIDC.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:08.520
The issuer URL, or
the URL of ADFS,

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.740
is used by the
Kubernetes API Server

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:13.710
to discover public keys
for verifying tokens.

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:15.960
The client ID is the
client application ID

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:19.110
used to make authentication
requests to ADFS.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:22.530
The client secret is used by the
G Cloud CLI and Cloud Console

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:25.650
to exchange authentication
codes for an ID token.

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:28.650
The username and group
specifies claims in the ID token

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:30.790
to use as username in groups.

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:33.390
There are a few other fields
such as Scopes, Extra Perams,

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:35.460
and CA Path, that
can be specified

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:38.370
based on your configuration
and identity provider.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:40.140
Deploy Cloud
Console Proxy should

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:42.809
be set to True if your
Open ID provider is not

00:08:42.809 --> 00:08:44.860
reachable over the
public internet.

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:48.090
This employs a reverse proxy
in the Kubernetes cluster.

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:50.850
After creating a user cluster
with this configuration,

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:53.490
Charlie can create
Kubernetes RBAC policies,

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:56.430
allowing the platform developer
group an active directory

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.590
to view pods in all name spaces.

00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:01.350
You can create a cluster role
and cluster role binding.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.240
Charlie needs to create
a login configuration

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:06.240
file containing details
about one or more clusters

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:07.890
and provide each
file to developers

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:09.400
who want to authenticate.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:11.700
You can do this using
the command gkectrl

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.790
create-login-config and pass
in the kube config for the user

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:15.792
cluster.

00:09:15.792 --> 00:09:16.500
Let's try it out.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:24.150
By default, this creates
a config file called

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:26.490
kubtctrl-anthos-config.yaml.

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:30.120
Let's take a look at this file.

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:33.240
This configuration file contains
the OIDC information provided

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:35.220
during cluster
creation, as well as

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.990
information about the
Kubernetes API server.

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:39.630
The file can be
distributed to Alex

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:41.550
and used to login to clusters.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:44.010
Alex and Blake can
access this file,

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:45.880
distribute it
through a secure URL,

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:48.920
and store in a well-known path.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:51.740
Once Alex has access to a
login configuration file,

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:55.450
Alex can login to clusters to
perform kube control commands.

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:59.040
Alex can login using the
GCloud CLI or cloud console.

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:01.520
The CLI experience starts
with kube control anthos

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:04.610
config file, with several
cluster definitions containing

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:07.870
information about the
identity provider in OIDC

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:10.070
There are two clusters
available, one of which

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:13.280
is configured with
ADFS for GKE on-prem.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:16.580
Alex can access the API
server using the GCloud CLI

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:19.040
with the gcloud
anthos auth command.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:21.682
Google Cloud identity
credentials are not required,

00:10:21.682 --> 00:10:23.390
and the component can
be installed simply

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:25.730
by running GCloud
components install

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:28.310
and updating to the
latest GCloud version.

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:30.020
The input is a
kube control anthos

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:32.540
config login configuration,
and the output

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:35.120
is a kube config, which
will be used by kube control

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.880
to send requests to the
Kubernetes API server,

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:40.070
containing IDN refresh tokens.

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:42.140
This will create a
local kube config

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:43.550
in the current directory.

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:48.480
Let's run gcloud anthos
auth login to log in.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:50.767
If Alex is out-passing
the list of clusters,

00:10:50.767 --> 00:10:52.975
the command will exit and
print the available options

00:10:52.975 --> 00:10:55.280
and request the user to
specify the cluster they

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:56.810
want to login to.

00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:59.000
Let's login to the ADFS cluster.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:01.610
It's the same command
as before, except now we

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:05.210
pass in a cluster argument
specifying the cluster name.

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:07.430
We'll perform an
open ID credential

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:10.250
exchange, starting a
connection to the IDP,

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:12.220
to allow Alex to login.

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:15.210
The IDP will return
an authorization code,

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:17.810
which we will take
and send to the IDP,

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:19.820
and get an IDN refresh token.

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:21.680
These tokens are
written to a kube config

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:24.320
file used by kube control.

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:26.810
We have a user account
under Alex's name

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:28.550
and we've made it
part of a group that

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:31.670
is defined in Active Directory
under platform developer.

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:34.860
We've created our backs and
applied them to our API server.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:37.285
You can see a kube config
file has been generated,

00:11:37.285 --> 00:11:39.410
so let's take a quick look
at what this kube config

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:40.025
file contains.

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:45.960
As you can see, we have
a kube config with an ID

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:48.520
and a refresh token
generated for our user.

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:50.690
If we run a command
using kube control

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:53.030
and the kube config
to get pods, it

00:11:53.030 --> 00:11:56.460
will return a list of the
running pods on the cluster.

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:57.210
So let's try that.

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:05.150
So let's take a look at
how Alex can authenticate

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:06.680
using the cloud console.

00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:10.160
Alex can navigate to the
cluster section in cloud console

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:13.280
and look at a list of available
clusters who logged in.

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:16.220
We previously logged
into ADFS to GKE on-prem.

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:18.680
Now let's try Okta with AWS.

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:20.090
So let's click on login.

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:22.790
And there's three
available login options.

00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:24.980
Token, basic auth,
and authenticate

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:27.210
using an external
identity provider.

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:30.570
So let's try the third one.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:33.910
This redirects us to an Okta
login page where we can log in.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:39.780
And as you can see,
Alex has successfully

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:43.320
logged into the
cluster running on AWS.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:45.330
We currently support
OIDC authentication

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:47.340
with third party
identity providers

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:49.583
for GKE on-prem and AWS.

00:12:49.583 --> 00:12:51.000
In the future, we
will add support

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:54.900
for additional environments,
such as GCP and other clouds.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:56.970
PRADEEP SAWLANI: As you
just saw in the demo,

00:12:56.970 --> 00:12:59.763
Charlie's organization
administrator uses ADFS

00:12:59.763 --> 00:13:01.920
with Active Directory.

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:05.130
Provisioning ADFS acts to
the on boarding friction,

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:07.410
as organization
administrators may have

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:09.780
vessels of funding constraints.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:12.060
Or maybe, because of
compliance reasons,

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:15.300
provisioning ADFS us may
not even be an option.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:17.460
In addition, there
may be a hidden cost

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:21.610
associated with managing and
mandating an ADFS server.

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:23.910
What if Anthos
supports authentication

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:27.210
with Active Directory using
LDAP or Kerberos instead

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:30.320
of provisioning ADFS.

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:32.260
Let's see how that
works with the demo.

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:36.840
NARRATOR: In this
demo, we will walk

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:39.480
through how to configure
LDAP with Anthos GKE

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:41.040
on-prem cluster.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:46.410
LDAP support was added in Anthos
1.4.1 and is in alpha state.

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:49.410
It is assumed Charlie has
created an admin workstation

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:52.080
and admin cluster,
and has reached out

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:54.410
to the organization
administrator for LDAP

00:13:54.410 --> 00:13:56.340
configuration.

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:58.620
Charlie creates user
cluster configuration

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:00.570
using gkectl command-line 2.

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:10.630
For our demo, we have
pre-filled configurations

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:15.100
specific to our environment,
which we will be using.

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:19.410
Let's take a look at the user
cluster configuration file.

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:24.480
We have named our cluster
as gke-onprem-ldap.

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:27.480
All fields related
to authentication

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:30.000
are under the
authentication anchor.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.000
Because Charlie
is not using ADFS,

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:35.910
the OIDC section
is kept disabled.

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:40.050
If needed, OIDC and LDAP can
be enabled simultaneously.

00:14:42.760 --> 00:14:46.130
The LDAP section is enabled
under the LDAP anchor.

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:48.560
The name represents the
human relabeled name

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:51.230
of the LDAP server.

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:55.160
The host field is provided by
the organization administrator

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:58.368
and is typically the IP address
of the host name for Active

00:14:58.368 --> 00:14:59.660
Directory domain name services.

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:08.170
At alpha we only support
simple LDAP connection type.

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:12.240
Support for stock TLS and LDAPS
will be added in the future.

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:19.690
The baseDN represents
bare user entry accessed

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:21.230
in the Active Directory.

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:27.120
The user attribute is the
name of the attribute which

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:29.670
precedes the username in DN.

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:31.440
The only supported
value at alpha

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:35.230
is CN, which
represents common name.

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:38.490
Member attribute is the name
of the attribute which records

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:39.940
a user group membership.

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:41.680
For Active Directory,
this is typically

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:44.094
the memberOf attribute.

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:50.900
After filling in all
qualification fields,

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:54.300
Charlie creates a user
cluster, gke-onprem-ldap,

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:55.640
using gkectl 2.

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:04.900
This step will take
some time to complete.

00:16:10.850 --> 00:16:12.730
Once a user cluster
is created, you

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:15.040
can check if LDAP is
configured correctly

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:17.820
by checking the status of
Anthos identity service.

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:35.050
Charlie creates our back
policy to allow users

00:16:35.050 --> 00:16:38.400
in the Active Directory security
group platform developers view

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:40.080
various Kubernetes resources.

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:47.090
As you can see,
the group over here

00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:50.540
represents the Active Directory
security group platform

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:51.290
developers.

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:02.640
Charlie then generates
a login configuration

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:05.369
for the clusters using
gkectl command-line 2.

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:21.329
Charlie then proceeds to
share this login configuration

00:17:21.329 --> 00:17:21.960
with Alex.

00:17:29.080 --> 00:17:33.550
Alex installs GCloud SDK
and uses GCloud anthos

00:17:33.550 --> 00:17:36.690
auth login command to get a kube
config for the user cluster.

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:46.140
Note that the argument
to the login config

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:47.940
is the file which was
shared by Charlie.

00:17:54.630 --> 00:17:57.620
Since we are logging
into an AD server,

00:17:57.620 --> 00:17:59.790
we will select option 1.

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:07.100
This is the username in the LDAP
and the corresponding password.

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:14.890
Once successful, this
command will generate

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:16.840
a kube config for Alex to use.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:22.540
Because Alex is a member of
the platform developers group,

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:26.540
he should be able to
view community resources.

00:18:26.540 --> 00:18:28.870
Let's check that out
with the kubectl command.

00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:39.270
This completes our
demo on how LDAP can be

00:18:39.270 --> 00:18:41.983
used with GKE on-prem clusters.

00:18:41.983 --> 00:18:43.400
PRADEEP SAWLANI:
Let's take a look

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:46.490
at how everything fits together.

00:18:46.490 --> 00:18:48.860
On the front end,
Alex can authenticate

00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:52.290
using CLI with GCloud
Anthos auth login,

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:55.970
or with cloud console by
navigating to the login button.

00:18:55.970 --> 00:18:57.770
On the back end,
we have a service

00:18:57.770 --> 00:19:01.670
running in the cluster called
Anthos identity service, which

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:04.960
is configured as a web
app token authenticator.

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:07.520
This service enables support
for different authentication

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:10.700
protocols, such
as OIDC and LDAP.

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:13.440
The front end works
alongside the back end

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:16.325
by writing tokens from
the identity provider

00:19:16.325 --> 00:19:18.200
and generating a
kube config file

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:21.980
with the appropriate format
for the banket to consume.

00:19:21.980 --> 00:19:24.110
In the future, we
will be adding support

00:19:24.110 --> 00:19:26.960
for additional authentication
protocols in the Anthos

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:29.200
identity service.

00:19:29.200 --> 00:19:32.320
We have spent some time talking
about user authentication

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:36.020
to Anthos GKE clusters,
but what about workloads

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:38.830
running on Anthos clusters?

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:41.350
Kubernetes workloads
aren't self-contained.

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:43.660
They often need to
authenticate to services

00:19:43.660 --> 00:19:45.070
outside of a cluster.

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:47.940
For example, Charlie's
teams may have a workload

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:50.770
that beat SS to a GCS bucket.

00:19:50.770 --> 00:19:53.200
How can you best secure
a workload accessing

00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:55.700
a GCP API and services?

00:19:55.700 --> 00:19:57.070
Let's take a closer look.

00:19:57.070 --> 00:19:59.830
Today, you can use
service account keys

00:19:59.830 --> 00:20:01.870
and store them as secrets.

00:20:01.870 --> 00:20:05.730
Workload can use these secrets
to access the GCS booking.

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:10.420
But these secrets are difficult
to deploy, audit, and manage.

00:20:10.420 --> 00:20:14.290
What if each workload could use
its built-in Kubernetes native

00:20:14.290 --> 00:20:17.230
identity that I,
Kubernetes service account,

00:20:17.230 --> 00:20:19.480
to authenticate to GCP?

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:22.070
Workload identity
for GKE enables

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:25.030
Kubernetes workloads, or
straight on any cluster,

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:28.330
to authenticate to Google
Cloud in a consistent way

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:31.270
without the use of
service account keys.

00:20:31.270 --> 00:20:33.790
This currently
works for GKE on GCP

00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:36.970
and will expand to other
environments like the AWS

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:39.510
or on-prem in the future.

00:20:39.510 --> 00:20:42.124
Let's see it in action.

00:20:42.124 --> 00:20:44.030
NARRATOR: In this
demo, we will walk

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:47.630
through how to enable workload
identity on an Anthos cluster

00:20:47.630 --> 00:20:52.800
on GCP and use workload
identity to access Google Cloud

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:56.520
API without downloading
a service account key.

00:20:56.520 --> 00:21:00.030
First, Charlie creates
an Anthos cluster on GCP

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:03.570
with workload identity enabled
with GCloud command-line 2.

00:21:06.780 --> 00:21:09.710
Note, the argument to
workload-pool option

00:21:09.710 --> 00:21:12.300
has to be our format
project ID followed

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:16.140
by the suffix svc.id.goog.

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:20.790
In Charlie's case, the
project ID is onprem-idp-test.

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:23.115
This step will take a couple
of minutes to complete.

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:34.450
After creating a cluster
with workload identity,

00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:36.440
Charlie creates a
Google service account,

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:38.590
which will be used
to access GCS bucket.

00:21:44.270 --> 00:21:47.870
Charlie configures iam policy
to allow service account

00:21:47.870 --> 00:21:52.620
gsa-app1 to access the
bucket app1 artifacts using

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:54.200
TSU kit command-line 2.

00:22:01.750 --> 00:22:05.930
Next, Charlie configures kubectl
to access the Anthos cluster.

00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:20.420
Next, Charlie
creates a namespace

00:22:20.420 --> 00:22:22.070
for the workload called app1.

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:37.890
Every namespace in Kubernetes
has a corresponding default

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:41.490
Kubernetes service account
associated with it.

00:22:41.490 --> 00:22:45.150
Charlie creates an iam policy
binding to allow the default

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:47.310
service account for
the namespace app1

00:22:47.310 --> 00:22:49.823
to use the Google
service account gsa-app1.

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:09.210
After this step, Charlie adds
annotation to the Kubernetes

00:23:09.210 --> 00:23:10.250
service account.

00:23:23.310 --> 00:23:26.960
Charlie now deploys sample
workload in the app1 namespace

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:30.955
to verify if the workload in
app1 one can access GCS bucket.

00:23:38.410 --> 00:23:41.890
In this case, the sample
workload is GCloud

00:23:41.890 --> 00:23:51.410
cloud-sdk:slim.

00:23:51.410 --> 00:23:54.220
The bucket now should give
us an interactive shell

00:23:54.220 --> 00:23:56.139
with cloak SDK installed.

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:07.050
Now Charlie will use
the gsutil command

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:08.730
to view to the
bucket containments.

00:24:21.900 --> 00:24:25.590
This demonstrates port users
Google service accounts

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:27.550
when calling Google Cloud API.

00:24:31.550 --> 00:24:34.460
PRADEEP SAWLANI: As you can
see, Anthos identity service,

00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:37.580
along with workload identity,
offers a seamless solution

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:40.610
for managing authentication
through multiple Anthos GKE

00:24:40.610 --> 00:24:43.760
environments, supporting
users and workloads

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:47.000
and providing a seamless
on boarding experience.

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:50.000
As a result, you can
get started quickly

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:53.030
with the product that has
built-in enterprise grade

00:24:53.030 --> 00:24:54.320
security.

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:57.110
You can save costs and
streamline your identity

00:24:57.110 --> 00:25:01.040
configuration by leveraging
existing identity providers.

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:04.320
Authentication doesn't have
to be complex or insecure.

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:07.430
Simplify identity with
Anthos identity today.

00:25:07.430 --> 00:25:09.250
Thank you.

